X:1
T:Good Ship Kangaroo, The
C:
M:4/4
L:1/8
K:C
%From the Christy Moore Songbook
%Collected from Elisabeth Cronin, Macroom, County Cork
%source: digitrad
%
GG | "C" G2 G2 D2 E2 | "Dm" F2 F2 F2>G2 | "F" A2 A2 "G" B2 B2 | "F" c6 dd |
w: Oh I ne-ver thought she would prove false or ei-her be un-true, As I
"C" e2 c2 c2 G2 | "F" A2 G2 E2 G2 | "G" G2>F2 D2 B,2 | "C" C4 z2 ||
w: sailed a-way through Mil-ford Bay on board the Kan-ga-roo.
%
W: Chorus:
W: Oh I never thought she would prove false or either prove untrue,
W: As we sailed away through Milford Bay on board the Kangaroo.
W:
W: 1. Once I was a waitin' man that lived at home at ease.
W: Now I am a mariner that ploughs the angry seas.
W: I always loved seafarin' life, I bid my love adieu
W: I shipped as steward and cook, me boys, on board the Kangaroo.
W: (Chorus)
W:
W: 2. "Think of me, oh think of me," she mournfully did say,
W: "When you are in a foreign land and I am far away.
W: Take this lucky tuppenny bit, it'll make you bear in mind
W: That lovin' trustin' faithful heart you left in tears behind."
W: (Chorus)
W:
W: 3. "Cheer Up, cheer up, my own true love. Don't weep so bitterly,"
W: She sobbed, she sighed, she choked, she cried and could not say goodbye
W: "I won't be gone for very long, 'tis but a month or two.
W: When I will return again of course I'll visit you."
W: (Chorus)
W:
W: 4. Our ship it was homeward bound from many's the foreign shore
W: And many's the foreign present unto me love I bore.
W: I brought tortoises from Tenerife and toys from Timbuktu
W: A china rat, a Bengal cat and a Bombay cockatoo.
W: (Chorus)
W:
W: 5. Paid off I sought her dwellin' on a street above the town
W: Where an ancient dame upon the line was hangin' out her gown.
W: "Where is me love?" "She's vanished, sir, si months ago
W: With a smart young man that drives the van for Chaplin, Son and Co.
W: (Chorus)
W:
W: 6. Here's a health to dreams of married life, to soap suds and blue,
W: Heart's true love and patent starch and washin' soda too.
W: I'll go unto some foreign shore, no longer can I stay
W: And with some China hottentot I'll throw meself away.
W: (Chorus)
W:
W: 7. Me love she is no foolish girl, her age it is two score
W: Me love she is no spinster, she's been married twice before.
W: I cannot say it was her wealth that stole me heart away;
W: She's a washer in a laundry for one and nine a day.
W: (Chorus)
%
